---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro"
---

<DocsLayout
  title="CapRover deployment"
  path="/docs/self-hosting/caprover"
  description="Deploy KoAkademy on CapRover as separate apps for PostgreSQL, Redis, Gotenberg, and the application."
>
  <p>
    CapRover runs each service as its own app on an internal Docker overlay
    network, with a built-in Nginx proxy and one-click Let's Encrypt. This guide
    creates four apps: PostgreSQL, Redis, Gotenberg, and KoAkademy itself.
    Services reach each other by hostname using CapRover's
    <code>srv-captain--&lt;app&gt;</code> convention.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A CapRover installation (dashboard on port <code>3000</code>) with a
      captain root domain and a wildcard DNS record for <code
        >*.apps.&lt;root&gt;</code
      >
    </li>
    <li>A stable KoAkademy release tag</li>
    <li>An external S3-compatible bucket for uploads</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>1. Databases</h2>
  <p>
    In the CapRover dashboard, create two apps from the one-click apps list:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>PostgreSQL</strong> — set a strong database password during creation
      (it cannot be changed from the dashboard afterwards). The app becomes reachable
      internally at <code>srv-captain--postgres</code> on port <code>5432</code
      >, and its data lives on its own persistent volume.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Redis</strong> — with a password if you set one; reachable at
      <code>srv-captain--redis</code> on port <code>6379</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Enable HTTPS on both apps and note the passwords for the next step.</p>

  <h2>2. Gotenberg</h2>
  <p>Create a custom app from the Gotenberg image:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Image: <code>gotenberg/gotenberg:8</code></li>
    <li>Container HTTP Port: <code>3000</code></li>
    <li>No environment variables required</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    KoAkademy reaches it internally at <code
      >http://srv-captain--gotenberg:3000</code
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>3. The KoAkademy app</h2>
  <p>Create a custom app for KoAkademy itself:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Image: <code>ghcr.io/yukazakiri/koakademy:&lt;latest-stable-tag&gt;</code>
    </li>
    <li>Container HTTP Port: <code>8000</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Set these environment variables in the app:</p>
  <pre><code>APP_ENV=production
APP_DEBUG=false
APP_KEY=
APP_URL=https://koakademy.apps.example.org
PORTAL_HOST=koakademy.apps.example.org
ADMIN_HOST=koakademy.apps.example.org
SESSION_SECURE_COOKIE=true
TRUSTED_PROXIES=*

DB_CONNECTION=pgsql
DB_HOST=srv-captain--postgres
DB_PORT=5432
DB_DATABASE=koakademy
DB_USERNAME=postgres
DB_PASSWORD=change-me

REDIS_HOST=srv-captain--redis
REDIS_PORT=6379
REDIS_PASSWORD=
CACHE_STORE=redis
SESSION_DRIVER=redis
QUEUE_CONNECTION=redis

GOTENBERG_URL=http://srv-captain--gotenberg:3000

AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=us-east-1
AWS_BUCKET=
AWS_ENDPOINT=
AWS_USE_PATH_STYLE_ENDPOINT=true</code></pre>
  <p>
    Replace the placeholder hostnames, credentials, and storage values with your
    own, and generate <code>APP_KEY</code> in the next step. In this topology the
    application is effectively stateless — uploads go to S3 and sessions, cache, and
    queues use Redis — so no persistent directory is required for the app. Attach
    one only if you need container logs or runtime workspace to survive a redeploy.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>TRUSTED_PROXIES=*</code> is acceptable here because only CapRover's internal
    Nginx can reach the application; see
    <a href="/docs/self-hosting/configuration">Configuration</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Migrations and setup</h2>
  <p>
    Make sure the PostgreSQL app has a database for KoAkademy. If the one-click
    template did not create one, create it from the server:
  </p>
  <pre><code>docker exec srv-captain--postgres createdb -U postgres koakademy</code></pre>
  <p>
    Set a stable application key: generate one once with
    <code
      >docker exec -it srv-captain--koakademy php artisan key:generate --show</code
    >
    and paste the complete value into <code>APP_KEY</code> in the dashboard. A key
    written only to the container's <code>.env</code> would be lost on the next redeploy.
    Then run the migration inside the application container:
  </p>
  <pre><code>docker exec -it srv-captain--koakademy php artisan migrate --force</code></pre>
  <p>
    In the dashboard, enable HTTPS for the app and force HTTP to HTTPS
    redirects. Visit <code>https://koakademy.apps.example.org/setup</code> to create
    the institution, initial academic period, and first administrator, then verify
    <code>/admin</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Backups</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Dump the PostgreSQL app's volume regularly:
      <code
        >docker exec srv-captain--postgres pg_dump -U postgres koakademy &gt;
        koakademy.sql</code
      >, and push the dump offsite. Redis is operational state, not the source
      of record.
    </li>
    <li>Back up the S3 upload bucket with the provider's controls.</li>
    <li>
      Preserve <code>APP_KEY</code> and the database password in a secret store.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Upgrades</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the release notes and take a database backup.</li>
    <li>Update the app's image to the new stable tag and redeploy.</li>
    <li>
      Run <code
        >docker exec -it srv-captain--koakademy php artisan migrate --force</code
      >.
    </li>
    <li>
      Smoke-test the portal, <code>/admin</code>, uploads, a queued job, and a
      PDF export.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The upgrade and rollback rules in
    <a href="/docs/self-hosting/deployment">Deployment</a> apply unchanged.
  </p>

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the app logs and
    <a href="/docs/self-hosting/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>. Common
    CapRover-specific causes: a missing or rotated <code>APP_KEY</code>, a
    database password that does not match the one-click app, and internal
    hostnames that do not match the <code>srv-captain--&lt;app&gt;</code>
    convention.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
